---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { registerUrl } from '../consts';
import '../styles/docs.css';

const sections = [
    ['account', 'Create an account'],
    ['smtp', 'Point your app at Wau'],
    ['send', 'Send a test message'],
    ['api', 'REST API'],
    ['mcp', 'MCP server'],
    ['production', 'Going to production'],
];

const mcpJson = `{
  <span class="k">"mcpServers"</span>: {
    <span class="k">"wau"</span>: {
      <span class="k">"type"</span>: <span class="v">"http"</span>,
      <span class="k">"url"</span>: <span class="v">"https://console.waumail.my/mcp/wau"</span>,
      <span class="k">"headers"</span>: { <span class="k">"Authorization"</span>: <span class="v">"Bearer \${WAU_TOKEN}"</span> }
    }
  }
}`;
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Getting started with Wau — capture your first email"
    description="Capture your first email with Wau: SMTP settings, a test message, the REST API and the MCP server."
    image="/og/docs.png"
    imageAlt="Wau docs: capture your first email in 5 minutes."
    current="docs"
>
    <main id="main" class="wrap docs">
        <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Documentation">
            <h2>Getting started</h2>
            {sections.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}
        </nav>

        <article class="prose">
            <p class="label-mono">Getting started · 5 minutes</p>
            <h1 style="margin-top:8px">Capture your first email</h1>
            <p style="margin-top:16px;font-size:var(--text-lg)">
                Wau is an SMTP server that accepts mail and never delivers it. You change your app's mail settings, send as usual, and read every
                message in the console.
            </p>

            <h2 id="account">1. Create an account</h2>
            <p>
                <a href={registerUrl('docs-step')}>Create an account at console.waumail.my</a>. A personal workspace and your first inbox are created for
                you. The Free plan needs no card.
            </p>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/register.png"><img src="/screenshots/register.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Wau console sign-up form with name, email address and password fields." /></a>
                <figcaption>Sign up at console.waumail.my.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="smtp">2. Point your app at Wau</h2>
            <p>Open the inbox and choose <strong>SMTP</strong>. Copy the values into your app's environment:</p>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/inbox.png"><img src="/screenshots/inbox.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Wau console showing the inbox list, the messages in the selected inbox and the SMTP button." /></a>
                <figcaption>Your inboxes, grouped by status. The <strong>SMTP</strong> button opens the inbox settings.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/smtp-settings.png"><img src="/screenshots/smtp-settings.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="SMTP settings panel listing host, port, username, password, auth and TLS, with a Laravel .env snippet." /></a>
                <figcaption>Host, port, username and password for the inbox, with ready-to-paste snippets for Laravel, swaks, Node.js and Python.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="code">
                <div class="code-head"><span>.env</span><button class="btn" type="button" data-copy="env-snippet"><Icon name="copy" /><span data-label>Copy</span></button></div>
<pre id="env-snippet" tabindex="0"><span class="k">MAIL_MAILER</span>=smtp
<span class="k">MAIL_HOST</span>=<span class="v">smtp.waumail.my</span>
<span class="k">MAIL_PORT</span>=<span class="v">2525</span>
<span class="k">MAIL_USERNAME</span>=<span class="v">&lt;inbox username&gt;</span>
<span class="k">MAIL_PASSWORD</span>=<span class="v">&lt;inbox password&gt;</span>
<span class="k">MAIL_ENCRYPTION</span>=<span class="v">null</span></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="callout">
                <Icon name="info" />
                <p><code>MAIL_ENCRYPTION=null</code> is intentional. The listener does not offer STARTTLS; TLS is handled in front of it. Do not change it to <code>tls</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <h2 id="send">3. Send a test message</h2>
            <p>From a Laravel app:</p>
            <div class="code">
                <div class="code-head"><span>tinker</span></div>
<pre tabindex="0"><span class="k">Mail</span>::raw(<span class="v">'Hello from staging'</span>, fn (<span class="k">$m</span>) =&gt; <span class="k">$m</span>-&gt;to(<span class="v">'qa@example.com'</span>)-&gt;subject(<span class="v">'Wau test'</span>));</pre>
            </div>
            <p>
                The message appears in the inbox within a few seconds. Open it to see the HTML, text, raw source, headers, attachments,
                authentication results and spam score.
            </p>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/message.png"><img src="/screenshots/message.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="A captured message open in the console, showing sender, recipient, authentication badges and the HTML preview." /></a>
                <figcaption>A captured message: sender, recipient, SPF/DKIM/DMARC results and the rendered HTML.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/message-headers.png"><img src="/screenshots/message-headers.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The Headers tab of a captured message." /></a>
                <figcaption>The <strong>Headers</strong> tab lists every header the message arrived with.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/message-spam.png"><img src="/screenshots/message-spam.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The Spam &amp; links tab showing a spam score of 1.4 out of 10 and a list of links." /></a>
                <figcaption><strong>Spam &amp; links</strong> shows the spam score against the 5.0 threshold and every link in the message.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="api">4. Read mail from tests with the REST API</h2>
            <p>Create a token under <strong>Settings → API tokens</strong>. Tokens start with <code>wau_</code> and are tied to one workspace.</p>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/api-tokens.png"><img src="/screenshots/api-tokens.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="API tokens settings page with a create-token form, a token list and a curl example." /></a>
                <figcaption>Create, review and revoke tokens. The full token is shown once, when it is created.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="code">
                <div class="code-head"><span>curl</span></div>
<pre tabindex="0"><span class="k">curl</span> https://console.waumail.my/api/sandboxes \
  -H <span class="v">"Authorization: Bearer $WAU_TOKEN"</span> \
  -H <span class="v">"X-API-Version: v1"</span></pre>
            </div>
            <table class="data">
                <thead><tr><th scope="col">Limit</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>General requests</td><td class="num">120 / minute per token</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Send and batch</td><td class="num">60 / minute per token</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Batch size</td><td class="num">500 messages</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h2 id="mcp">5. Optional: read mail from your coding agent</h2>
            <p>
                Wau runs an MCP server at <code>https://console.waumail.my/mcp/wau</code>. It uses the same API token and the same workspace
                roles as the console: any member can list inboxes and read messages; members and above can create inboxes, send test
                messages, forward and delete messages; clearing an inbox needs admin; editing, rotating credentials or deleting an inbox needs
                owner. Add it to your project's <code>.mcp.json</code>:
            </p>
            <div class="code">
                <div class="code-head"><span>.mcp.json</span></div>
                <pre tabindex="0" set:html={mcpJson} />
            </div>
            <p>Then ask your agent things like "show the last password-reset email in shopfront-staging".</p>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/mcp.png"><img src="/screenshots/mcp.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="MCP connection settings page showing the server address and the token picker." /></a>
                <figcaption><strong>Settings → MCP connection</strong> shows the server address and which token to use.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="shot">
                <a href="/screenshots/mcp-config.png"><img src="/screenshots/mcp-config.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="MCP connection page showing the .mcp.json snippet and a sample prompt." /></a>
                <figcaption>Copy the <code>.mcp.json</code>, <code>claude mcp add</code> or shell snippet for your client.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="production">6. Going to production</h2>
            <p>
                Production is the reverse of step 2: point the same mail settings at your real provider. Wau never delivers mail and is never in
                the production path, so keep its credentials in development, staging and UAT environments only.
            </p>

            <div class="next">
                <a class="card" href={registerUrl('docs-next')}><span>Next</span>Create your account</a>
                <a class="card" href="/pricing"><span>Plans</span>Compare plans and limits</a>
            </div>
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
